NAME
       make - build what is out of date

SYNOPSIS
       make [-f makefile] [-C dir] [-nskiBq] [VAR=value] [target...]

DESCRIPTION
       Reads a makefile, works out which targets are older than what
       they are built from, and runs the recipes that bring them up to
       date. With no target named it builds the first one the makefile
       defines.

       Recipe lines begin with a tab and are handed to sh(1), one line
       per shell, so a recipe that needs several commands to share a
       directory joins them with && or \ the way it would anywhere
       else.

RULES
       A rule is targets, a colon, the things they are built from, and
       a recipe indented with tabs:

         app: main.o util.o
                 $(CC) -o $@ $^

       % in a target makes a pattern rule that matches any name, with
       the part % stood for available as $*:

         %.o: %.c
                 $(CC) $(CFLAGS) -c -o $@ $<

       Prerequisites after a | are order-only: they are built first but
       do not make the target out of date. .PHONY names targets that
       are not files, so they run every time.

       Pattern rules for .c, .s and .S objects and for linking a single
       object or source are built in, and are tried only after the ones
       the makefile defines.

VARIABLES
       = defines a variable expanded each time it is used, := expands
       it once at that point, += appends and ?= sets it only if it has
       no value yet. A variable named on the command line wins over the
       makefile. Anything not defined falls back to the environment.

       $(VAR:.c=.o) rewrites the end of every word.

       Inside a recipe $@ is the target, $< the first prerequisite, $^
       all of them, $? those newer than the target, and $* the stem the
       pattern matched.

FUNCTIONS
       subst patsubst strip findstring filter filter-out sort word
       words wordlist firstword lastword dir notdir suffix basename
       addprefix addsuffix join wildcard shell if foreach error
       warning info

CONDITIONALS
       ifeq, ifneq, ifdef and ifndef with else and endif, and include,
       -include and sinclude for pulling in another makefile.

OPTIONS
       -f FILE  read FILE rather than GNUmakefile, makefile or Makefile
       -C DIR   change to DIR before doing anything
       -n       print the recipes without running them
       -s       run the recipes without printing them
       -k       carry on with other targets after one fails
       -i       ignore a failing recipe line everywhere
       -B       rebuild regardless of the timestamps
       -q       print nothing; exit 1 if something needs remaking

       A recipe line starting with @ is not printed, one starting with
       - may fail without stopping the build.

LIMITS
       Timestamps are compared to the second, so a file written in the
       same second as the target it is built from does not count as
       newer. There is no -j: recipes run one at a time.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), gcc(1)
